WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.392
Effective group work needs more than a shared screen.

2
00:00:03.592 --> 00:00:10.781
Give the group a common outcome and roles that rotate: a builder arranges the claim, an evidence checker challenges

3
00:00:10.981 --> 00:00:14.406
the support and an explainer justifies the connection.

4
00:00:14.606 --> 00:00:17.357
Everyone should have a meaningful contribution.

5
00:00:17.557 --> 00:00:25.114
When live collaboration is unavailable, use turn-taking at one display or exchange saved copies without pretending

6
00:00:25.314 --> 00:00:27.700
the files are simultaneously shared.

7
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:31.934
Model useful questions before asking students to collaborate.

8
00:00:32.134 --> 00:00:39.370
Ask which observation supports the claim, what alternative explanation might fit and what would change their mind.

9
00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.153
Give learners time to rehearse a response.

10
00:00:42.353 --> 00:00:46.129
Watch whether a confident student is making every decision.

11
00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:51.843
Adjust the roles or the task so that quieter learners also explain something important.

12
00:00:52.043 --> 00:00:56.366
End the activity with an individual explanation or a new connection.

13
00:00:56.566 --> 00:01:02.096
That evidence helps distinguish a strong group product from each learner’s understanding.

14
00:01:02.296 --> 00:01:06.890
Invite students to identify one revision that came from the discussion.

15
00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:14.164
Pause and add role cards, two discussion questions and one individual exit prompt to your board plan.

16
00:01:14.364 --> 00:01:18.987
Judge the quality of the reasoning rather than how much artwork the group produces.
